The '''Constitution of the Baliscan Confederation''' ({{lang-bl|Constitución de la Confederación Baliscana}}), popularly known as the '''Confederal Constitution''' ({{lang-bl|Constitución confederal}}) was enacted via [[2022 Baliscan constitutional referendum|popular referendum]] on 23 July 2022, with 69% of all votes cast in favor. 

This Constitution was drafted following the [[2021 Baliscan civil unrest]], and came into force on 30 July 2022.

== Content ==
The text of the Political Constitution of the Confederation begins with the preamble approved by the Constitutional Convention:

{{quote|{{lang|bl|Nosaltres y nosaltres, el pueblo de Balisca, conformado por diversas communida, nos otorgamos libremente esta Constitución, acordada en un proceso participativo, paritario y democrático.}}
{{clear}}
We, the people of Balisca, made up of various communities, freely grant ourselves this Constitution, agreed upon in a participatory, equal and democratic process.
|multiline=yes}}

The content is distributed in eleven chapters, added to a section of transitory regulations located at the end:

=== Basic Laws ===

Chapter I (articles 1 to <>) is the longest chapter of the constitutional proposal, stating that the end of rights is a dignified life, [[W:Democracy|democracy]], [[W:Peace|peace]] and the balance of nature, endowing people, [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous nations]] and nature as holders of [[W:Individual rights|individual]] and [[W:Collective rights|collective rights]], committing the action of the State to eliminate access barriers to these rights.

* Right to [[W:Security of person|personal integrity]] (physical, psychosocial, sexual and affective)
* Prohibition of the death penalty, torture, enforced disappearance, slavery, human trafficking and exile
* Right to truth, justice, memory and reparation in case of human rights violations
* Right to [[W:Right to protest|protest]]
* Right to [[W:Right to resist|resist]]
* Right to [[W:Equality before the law|equality before the law]]
* Right to [[W:Non-discrimination|non-discrimination]]
* Right to [[W:Children's rights|progressive autonomy]] of children and adolescents
* Right to the [[W:Self-identification|full development]] of the personality and the use of [[W:Linguistic rights|one's own language]]
* Right to [[W:Child protection|child protection]]
* Right to life and environment free of violence (for children, adolescents and people of [[W:Sexual diversity|sexual]] and [[W:Gender diversity|gender diversity]] and dissidence)
* Right to universal accessibility
* Respect for [[W:Neurodiversity|neurodiversity]]
* Right on request
* Right to dignified treatment, social reintegration and communication (in case of imprisonment)
* Right to a decent old age
* Right to education (including [[W:Sex education|sex education]])
* Right to [[W:Right to a healthy environment|care for the environment]]
* Right to freedom of education
* Right to health
* The right to [[W:Social security|social security]]
* Right to free choice of work, to [[W:Remuneration|equitable remuneration]], [[W:Freedom of association|freedom of association]], [[W:Labor rights|labor participation]] and [[W:Strike action|strike]]
* Recognition of domestic work
* Right to care
* Right to [[W:right to housing|decent housing]]
* Collective [[W:Right to the city|right to the city]] and territory
* Right to [[W:Food sovereignty|food sovereignty]]
* Right to [[W:Human right to water and sanitation|water and sanitation]]
* Right to energy
* Right to sport
* Sexual and [[W:Reproductive rights|reproductive rights]]
* Right to own worldview
* Right to [[W:Land Back|indigenous consent]]
* Right to [[W:Laicism|private freedom of worship]]
* Right to a [[W:Right to die|dignified death]]
* Right to [[W:Freedom of movement|freedom of movement]]
* Right to [[Basic Law: Protection of Privacy|privacy]]
* Right to asylum and refuge
* Right to [[W:Freedom of association|freedom of association]]
* Right to public demonstration
* Right to [[W:Open-source governance|access information from the State]]
* Right to [[W:Personal property|personal property]]
* Right to {{W|free love}} and [[W:Civil marriage|civil marriage]] (all forms)
* Right to [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|indigenous sovereignty]] and [[W:Indigenous decolonization|decolonization]]
* Right to freedom to undertake
* Right to [[W:Freedom of expression|freedom of expression]]
* Right to create social media
* Right to [[W:Right to Internet access|digital connectivity]], computer security, a digital space free of violence, along with education in the digital space
* Right to [[Cybersecurity and Privacy Initiative|personal data protection]]
* Right to [[W:Right to rest and leisure|rest and leisure]]
* Right to [[W:Cultural rights|cultural and artistic participation]]
* Right to read
* Right to research
* Right to [[W:Cultural rights|cultural heritage]]
* Right to regeneration of nature
* Right to a healthy and balanced environment
* Right to clean air
* [[W:Freedom to roam|Universal access]] to mountains, beaches, lakes, lagoons and wetlands
* Access to [[W:Right to a fair trial|justice and due process]]

In addition to functioning as a "[[W:Bill of rights|bill of rights]]," this chapter includes new uniform requirements to access Baliscan nationality and citizenship, recognizing the rights of persons residing abroad and those who have migrated, the right to guardianship for the reestablishment of violated rights or put an end to irrational arrests, compensation in the event of unjust arrest and imprisonment, and the principles of the "[[Ombudsman]]" as an entity for the promotion and protection of human rights guaranteed in this Constitution, in international human rights treaties ratified and in force in Balisca in conjunction with the "Children's Rights Ombudsman" whose purpose will be the protection of the aforementioned rights, but in childhood and adolescence.

=== General principles and provisions ===
[[File:Salto monument.png|thumb|In Chapter II, the Constitution establishes that Balisca, in its diversity, "forms a unique and indivisible territory", and established confederal symbols (i.e. the [[flag of Balisca|flag]], [[Coat of arms of Balisca|coat of arms]] and [[W:The Internationale|anthem]])]]

Chapter II (articles <> to <>), called ''general principles and provisions'', contains the fundamental principles of the formation of the [[Baliscan Confederation]], its role, its relationship with people, its territory distribution among others. It reaffirms [[social protection in Balisca]], its [[w:plurinationalism|plurinational]] and [[w:ecology|ecological]] character, the form of exercise of sovereignty, the protection of {{W|human rights}} as the basis for [[W:Minarchism|any and all confederal action]], the free association of the territory (recognizing [[W:Indigenous sovereignity|Indigenous sovereignity]]), the {{W|freedom}} and [[w:Equality before the law|equality]] of people and their relationship with nature, the recognition of participation and equitable representation of all diversities and sexual and gender dissidence in positions of power, {{W|secularism}}, and the recognition of all forms of family.

'''Article I: Fundamental Principles'''

'''Sovereignty:''' The sovereignty of the Baliscan/Anhangá-Quetrupillán people is paramount. All power derives from the people, who exercise their sovereignty through their {{W|popular assembly|local popular assemblies}}.

'''Liberation and equality:''' We stand for the complete liberation of Balisca from all forms of oppression, including imperialism, capitalism, and social injustice. We uphold the principle of equality, where all individuals are entitled to equal rights and opportunities, irrespective of gender, ethnicity, religion, or social status.

'''Popular participation:''' The Baliscan Confederation believes in direct democracy and the active participation of all members of society in decision-making processes. We promote the autonomy and self-management of communities, encouraging grassroots initiatives and bottom-up organization.

'''Social justice:''' The Baliscan Confederation is committed to [[W:Social justice|social justice]], aiming to eradicate poverty, inequality, and exploitation. We seek to redistribute wealth, ensure fair access to resources, and provide a dignified life for all citizens.

'''National liberation:''' The Baliscan Confederation recognizes the right of all communities within Balisca to self-determination, cultural preservation, and political representation. We reject any form of [[W:National chauvinism|national chauvinism]] or discrimination.

'''Article II: Structure of the [[Confederal government of Balisca|Confederal Government]]'''

'''Confederal Congress:''' The [[Confederal Congress]] serves as the highest level of democratic authority in the Baliscan Confederation. Composed of all local delegates, it deliberates on strategic objectives, policies, and major organizational matters.

'''Secretariat Council:''' The [[Secretariat Council (Balisca)|Secretariat Council]] is elected by the Confederal Congress and acts as the body responsible for implementing the decisions of the Congress. It coordinates and guides the activities of the regions, ensuring the realization of their common goals.

'''Local assemblies:''' Local assemblies are established at the communal level, promoting self-governance, [[W:Participatory democracy|participatory democracy]], and the needs of local populations. They have the authority to make decisions within their respective regions, ensuring that power is decentralized and that decisions are made at the most appropriate level.

'''People's defense:''' The [[Confederal Armed Forces]] are responsible for safeguarding the peoples' right to [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]] and defending the sovereignty of Balisca. They operate under the direction of the [[Defense Council (Balisca)|Defense Council]] and are accountable to the people, whom express their will directly, or through their delegate to the [[Confederal Congress]]. This includes the [[Autonomous Militia]].

'''Article III: Fundamental Rights and Freedoms'''

'''Civil and political rights:''' All individuals within the territory of the Baliscan/Anhangá-Quetrupillán archipelago have the right to freedom of expression, association, assembly, and political participation. They have the right to a fair and impartial trial, freedom from torture and cruel treatment, and protection of their personal privacy.

'''Economic and social rights:''' Guarantees the [[W:Right to an adequate standard of living|right to an adequate standard of living]], fair wages, and safe working conditions. The confederal government will ensure access to education, healthcare, housing, and social security for all citizens. The confederal government promotes cooperative ownership, collective decision-making, [[W:Regenerative economic theory|regenerativity]] and [[W:circular economy|circularity]].

'''Cultural and linguistic rights:''' Respect and protection for the cultural diversity and linguistic heritage of all communities within its territory. The confederal government recognizes the right to preserve, develop, and promote local cultures, languages, and traditional knowledge.

'''Article IV: Revolutionary Justice'''

'''Revolutionary justice system:''' Establishes a revolutionary and [[W:restorative justice|restorative justice]] system that is decentralized, transparent, and based on the principles of fairness, equality, and popular participation. It aims to rectify historical injustices, hold accountable those responsible for crimes against the people, and promote [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation]].

'''Truth and reconciliation commissions:''' Establishes court-like committees, with the stated purpose  to build a fair, democratic and inclusive society in Balisca. Through carefully selected engagements and interventions, the councils seek to shape communal, cantonal, and regional approaches to transitional justice and reconciliation in Balisca by drawing on community intelligence as well as macro-trend research and comparative analysis. These councils publish their research, make policy recommendations, and perform extensive reconciliation work on the ground. The Commission's findings and recommendations must always guide the pursuit of justice and reparations.

'''Article V: Territorial Integrity and International Relations'''

'''Territorial integrity:''' Commitment to the territorial integrity and acknowledges the interdependence of the Baliscan territories. It respects the rights of all regions within its territory and ensures their fair representation in decision-making processes.

'''International solidarity:''' Stands in solidarity with all peoples around the world who strive for liberation, social justice, and equality. It advocates for the rights of oppressed nations and supports movements that align with its revolutionary principles.

'''Article VI: Constitutional Amendments'''

This Constitution may be amended by a two-thirds majority vote of the Confederal Congress, provided that the proposed amendments align with the revolutionary principles and goals of this document.

